  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We will use a two-phase optimization strategy that combines global and local searches to minimize the maximum correlation. The core idea is to first find a globally optimal solution using a reduced discretization, then refine it by time scaling and interpolation. This differs from the standard approach by using a more aggressive gradient-free optimization and leveraging mathematical transformations to improve performance. The rationale is that by using fewer samples initially, we can find better candidates faster, then scale them up to achieve a lower upper bound.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Objective: Minimize \ud835\udc36_5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - Constraints: \n        - h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for all x\n        - \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n   - Representation: Discrete step function with n_equidistant grid points in [0,2]\n   - Method: Projected gradient descent with adaptive step sizing and dimensionality reduction\n   - Algorithm:\n        * Precompute correlations using fast Fourier transform (FFT)\n        * Use different lags for gradient approximation\n        * Project solutions to satisfy integrability constraints using iterative reweighting\n   - Init: Start with parent's values scaled to M samples (much fewer than 200)\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Set variables:\n        M = 10  # Reduced parent samples (good compromise)\n        n_points = 420  # Final target resolution\n        dx = 2.0/n_points\n   - Scale parent to M samples:\n        xs = np.linspace(0, 2, n_scale)  # Where n_scale is the parent's length or better value\n        ys = parent_values\n        xt = np.linspace(0, 2, M)\n        h_initial = np.interp(xt, xs, ys)\n   - Project to integrate to 1:\n        total_int = (2.0 / M) * sum(h_initial)\n        correction = (1 - total_int) / M  # Simple heuristic\n        h_initial += correction\n   - Compute initial correlation:\n        corr = np.correlate(h_initial, 1-h_initial, mode='full')\n        c5 = max(corr)*dx\n   - Iteration parameters:\n        n_epochs = 5000\n        epochs_per_scale = 200\n        lag_range = [-250, 250]  # Arithmetic with indices requires handling wrap-around properly\n   - Adaptivity mechanisms:\n        \u2022 Progressive dimension increase: Start with small n_scale and scale up\n        \u2022 Dynamic lag set selection: Focus on top k_lags indices during gradient descent\n        \u2022 Step-size adaptive adjustment: Base optimization on gradient magnitude\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Standard implementation:\n        n_scale = 64 (better parent sample reduction)\n        n_points = 500\n        max_lag = n_scale//4 (focused search, good for most cases)\n   - Variation 1 (low latency):\n        E.g., n_scale=32, n_points=256, fewer but cheaper samples\n   - Variation 2 (gradient sensitivity):\n        E.g., n_scale=128, n_points=700, better gradient quality\n\n5. Computation budget and robustness:\n   - Dominant cost: Correlation calculation O(n\u00b2) with FFT\n   - Typical runtime:\n        * lopsided = 5*somesteps * (M log M + 400*log 400)\n        * C estimate: 7*somesteps \u2248 7*0.5*someps* FFT_M + FFT_A*\n   -  246 iterations done in 60 seconds at 500ns/iteration (projected on 3.2GHz)\n   -  Typical 3 minute run for our M and target n_points\n   -  Check for early convergenceEvery 100 steps, monitor gradient Lipschitz\n   -  erturbation: Random walk with mean-free path proportional to grid spacing\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   -  onvergence criteria:\n        \u2022 Time < budget_s\n        \u2022 Gradient norm < 1e-12, c5 stopping threshold\n   -  Validation steps:\n        Calculate c5 for final solution\n        Check 0<=h<=1 for all points\n        Verify integral: (2/n_points)*sum(h) == 1 (within 1e-10 tolerance)\n   -  Safety checks:\n        Any attempt to leave [0,1] is automatically projected with safeguard\n        monitor temperaturein optimization (ensure local minima are properly explored)\n   -  Format output:\n        (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) as required\n   -  Fallback procedure:\n        Always return the initial solution if time runs out prematurely",
